{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-42497","model_call":17,"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ (+ guiyu china india ghana agbogbloshie) (+ \"e-waste\" \"electronic waste\") (+ burning \"open burning\") (+ air dioxins fumes emissions))"},"record":{"query":{"op":"query","query":"(^ (+ guiyu china india ghana agbogbloshie) (+ \"e-waste\" \"electronic waste\") (+ burning \"open burning\") (+ air dioxins fumes emissions))"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_08_1276221077#1_2305275296","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q1479","rank":1,"snippet":"components such as gold. Combustion from burning e-waste creates fine particulate matter, which is linked to pulmonary and cardiovascular disease. While the health implications of e-waste are difficult to isolate due to the informal working conditions, poverty, and poor sanitation, several studies in Guiyu, a city in southeastern China, offer insight. Guiyu is known as the largest e-waste recycling site in the world, and the city’s residents exhibit substantial digestive, neurological, respiratory, and bone problems. For example, 80 percent of Guiyu’s children experience respiratory ailments, and are especially at risk of lead poisoning. 3 Residents of Guiyu are not the only ones at risk. Researchers such as Brett Robinson, a professor of soil and physical sciences at Lincoln University in New Zealand, warn that wind patterns in Southeast <cover>China disperse toxic particles released by open-air burning across the Pearl River Delta Region, home to 45 million people.\" , \"start_char\": 868.000000 , \"title\": \"The Human and Environmental Effects of E-Waste – </cover>Population Reference Bureau\" , \"url\": \"http://www.prb.org/Publications/Articles/2013/e-waste.aspx\" }"},"time":259},"model_calls":17,"document_judgments":14,"relevance_3":11,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
