{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-42497","model_call":18,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1479"},"record":{"query":{"op":"next","qid":"q1479"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_19_1082813673#4_1510636196","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1479","rank":3,"snippet":"000000 , \"headings\": \"Why Is E-waste Being Shipped To Developing Countries? Why Is E-waste Being Shipped To Developing Countries? Why Companies Ship E-Waste to Developing Countries Safety & Environmental Regulations The Effects of Shipping E-Waste How to Prevent the Shipping of E-Waste \" , \"segment\": \"But, is it ethical? Safety & Environmental Regulations Most of the <cover>e-waste that is shipped is sent to China, Ghana, Nigeria, Pakistan, India, and Vietnam. Unlike the U.S., these countries do not have safety or environmental regulations in place to protect their people or the environment. For example, Jim Puckett, the executive director of the non-profit organization, Basel Action Network, has witnessed teenage orphans in Ghana burning electronics and releasing toxic fumes </cover>into the air. In Nigeria, he observed workers tossing electronic pieces that had no value into a pile and lighting it on fire. Besides harming the environment, these workers are also not given the safety gear that they need, such as goggles and a facemask, to protect themselves from exposure to these toxins. The Effects of Shipping E-Waste Toxics Link performed a study in India to test ","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_08_1276221077#0_2305273239","rank":2,"relevance":3}]},"time":8},"model_calls":18,"document_judgments":15,"relevance_3":12,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
