{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-42497","model_call":24,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1479"},"record":{"query":{"op":"next","qid":"q1479"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_19_1082813673#3_1510634348","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1479","rank":10,"snippet":"sort through the waste. The U.S. also has strict environmental and safety regulations that recyclers must abide by, which adds to the cost of operating the plant. Some recycling plants have found that shipping their hazardous e-waste overseas for processing is much less expensive than handling it in the states. But, is it ethical? Safety & Environmental Regulations Most of the e-waste that is shipped is sent to China, Ghana, Nigeria, Pakistan, India, and Vietnam. Unlike the U.S., these countries do not have safety or environmental regulations in place to protect their people or the environment. For example, Jim Puckett, the executive director of the non-profit organization, Basel Action Network, has witnessed teenage orphans in <cover>Ghana burning electronics and releasing toxic fumes into the air. In Nigeria, he observed workers tossing electronic pieces that had no value into a pile and lighting it on fire.\" , \"start_char\": 1915.000000 , \"title\": \"Why Is E-waste </cover>Being Shipped To Developing Countries? – ERI\" , \"url\": \"https://eridirect.com/blog/2017/06/why-is-e-waste-being-shipped-to-developing-countries/\" }","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_05_1690642284#3_3248061841","rank":9,"relevance":3}]},"time":8},"model_calls":24,"document_judgments":21,"relevance_3":18,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
