{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-42497","model_call":48,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1493"},"record":{"query":{"op":"next","qid":"q1493"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_17_458289946#4_496641536","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1493","rank":4,"snippet":"rural areas of western China, or through peddlers who re-sell e-wastes to dealers. The third channel is the most common form of e-waste management in China, which creates a massive informal sector. Treatment Most of this e-waste is sent to recycling sites in order to extract precious metals and organic materials to be resold for economic value. The most common form of e-waste treatment used in China is the “physical/mechanical method” that separates the difference elements in each electronic device through manual dismantling and chemical separation. This often involves rudimentary practices, such as heating circuit boards, cutting cables and wires, chipping and melting plastics, and extracting precious metals through acid leaching and incineration. These operations, such as strong acid leaching and the open <cover>burning of heavy materials, have resulted in the release of toxic metals and pollutants. Informal sector The majority of e-waste recycling in China takes place illegally within the informal sector.\" , \"start_char\": 3145.000000 , \"title\": \"Electronic waste </cover>in China - Wikipedia\" , \"url\": \"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Electronic_waste_in_China\" }"},"time":2},"model_calls":48,"document_judgments":38,"relevance_3":27,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
