{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-42497","model_call":60,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1500"},"record":{"query":{"op":"next","qid":"q1500"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_08_1276221077#0_2305273239","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1500","rank":4,"snippet":"in China, India, Pakistan, Vietnam, and the Philippines handle anywhere from 50 percent to 80 percent of this e-waste, often shredding, burning, and dismantling the products in “backyards.” Emissions from these recycling practices are damaging human health and the environment. 2 Developing countries with rapidly growing economies handle e-waste from developed countries, and from their own internal consumers. Currently, an estimated 70 percent of e-waste handled in <cover>India is from other nations, but the UNEP estimates that between 2007 and 2020, domestic television e-waste will double, computer e-waste will increase five times, and cell phones 18 times. The informal sector’s recycling practices magnify health risks. For example, primary and secondary exposure to toxic metals, such as lead, results mainly from open-air burning </cover>used to retrieve valuable components such as gold. Combustion from burning e-waste creates fine particulate matter, which is linked to pulmonary and cardiovascular disease.\" , \"start_char\": 0.000000 , \"title\": \"The Human and Environmental Effects of E-Waste – Population Reference Bureau\" , \"url\": \"http://www.prb.org/Publications/Articles/2013/e-waste.aspx\"","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_08_1276221077#0_2305273239","rank":4,"relevance":3}]},"time":8},"model_calls":60,"document_judgments":48,"relevance_3":29,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
